+<dt id="cvss">CVSS Vector</dt>
+<dd>
+<a href="https://www.first.org/cvss/calculator/3.1#CVSS:3.1/AV:A/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:N">CVSS:3.1/AV:A/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:N</a>
+</dd>
+<dt id="patched">Patched</dt>
+<dd>
+<ul>
+<li>
+<code>&#62;=4.2.1, &#60;5.0.0</code>
+</li>
+<li>
+<code>&#62;=5.2.0</code>
+</li>
+</ul>
+</dd>
+<dt id="unaffected">Unaffected</dt>
+<dd>
+<ul>
+<li>
+<code>&#60;0.64.0</code>
+</li>
+</ul>
+</dd>
+</dl>
+<h3 id="description">Description</h3>
+<p>With the <code>openssl-tls</code> connector, configuring a cluster CA (<code>certificate-authority</code>, <code>certificate-authority-data</code>, or the in-cluster <code>ca.crt</code>) did not limit which server certificates were trusted. The connector started from OpenSSL's default verify paths and added the configured roots to that store, so a server certificate chaining to any root in OpenSSL's default store passed trust validation. The certificate still had to be valid for the server name the client connects to. client-go and the <code>rustls-tls</code> connector trust only the configured CA.</p>
+<p>An attacker on the path between the client and the apiserver who presents such a certificate, for example from a TLS inspection proxy whose root is installed on the machine, could complete the handshake and receive the client's requests, including bearer tokens.</p>
+<p>This advisory covers the <code>openssl-tls</code> connector, added in 0.64.0, when a cluster CA is configured. The connector is used by <code>Client::try_default()</code>, <code>Client::try_from(Config)</code> and <code>ClientBuilder::try_from(Config)</code> whenever they select OpenSSL, by the <code>ConfigExt::openssl_https_connector</code>, <code>openssl_https_connector_with_connector</code> and <code>openssl_ssl_connector_builder</code> methods even when <code>rustls-tls</code> is also enabled, and since 4.2.0 for TLS to an <code>https://</code> proxy.</p>
+<p>Which connector the constructors select depends on the version. Since 0.86.0, <code>rustls-tls</code> is the default and takes precedence when both features are enabled. From 0.71.0 to 0.85.x, <code>openssl-tls</code> was a default feature of <code>kube</code> (and of <code>kube-client</code> up to 0.82.2) and took precedence over <code>rustls-tls</code>. The <code>kube</code> crate re-exports <code>kube-client</code>, so the same <code>kube</code> versions are affected.</p>
+<p>The flaw was corrected in 5.2.0 and 4.2.1 by installing a certificate store that holds only the configured root certificates. After upgrading, an <code>https://</code> proxy or a CA bundle that relied on OpenSSL's default store may need its CA added to the kubeconfig CA bundle. On older versions, switching the client to the <code>rustls-tls</code> connector avoids the issue.</p>
